[{"id":"30871","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>When did the twelfth five year plan of India commence?</span></p>","option_1":"1990","option_2":"1995","option_3":"2007","option_4":"2012","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - 2012.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Twelfth Five Year Plan of India, themed 'Faster, More Inclusive and Sustainable Growth', commenced in 2012 and ran until 2017.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It was the last of India's Five Year Plans, as the Planning Commission was subsequently replaced by NITI Aayog in 2015, and formal five-year planning was discontinued thereafter.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30872","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Todarmal was whose revenue minister who carried out a careful survey of crop yields?</span></p>","option_1":"Babur","option_2":"Humayun","option_3":"Akbar","option_4":"Shah Jahan","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Akbar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Raja Todar Mal served as the revenue minister (Diwan-i-Ashraf) under Emperor Akbar, and is best known for devising the Zabt/Dahsala land revenue settlement system, based on a careful survey of crop yields, land measurement, and average produce over ten years.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This system, known as Todar Mal's Bandobast, became one of the most efficient and lasting administrative reforms of the Mughal era.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30873","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following kingdoms was divided into administrative units known as 'Garh'?</span></p>","option_1":"Kushan Dynasty","option_2":"Maratha Empire","option_3":"Delhi Sultanate","option_4":"Gond Kingdom","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Gond Kingdom.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Gond Kingdom (in central India, present-day Madhya Pradesh/Chhattisgarh region) was traditionally organised into administrative units known as 'Garhs', each headed by a local chieftain.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This decentralised system of Garhs gave rise to the historical term 'Garha-Mandla' or 'Garha-Katanga', referring to the network of 52 (or more) Garhs that constituted the Gond kingdom.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30874","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>According to the Industrial Policy Statement, 1980, the investment limit in tiny units was raised to Rs. _____ lakhs.</span></p>","option_1":"1","option_2":"2","option_3":"5","option_4":"10","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 2.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Industrial Policy Statement of 1980 revised the investment ceiling for tiny units (a sub-category of small-scale industries) by raising it to Rs. 2 lakhs, in order to promote small and decentralised industrial units across the country.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This policy also emphasised the promotion of small-scale and cottage industries, and the correction of regional industrial imbalances.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30875","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>How many carbon atoms are present in one molecule of glucose?</span></p>","option_1":"3","option_2":"4","option_3":"5","option_4":"6","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - 6.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Glucose is a simple sugar (monosaccharide) with the molecular formula C6H12O6, meaning each molecule contains 6 carbon atoms, 12 hydrogen atoms, and 6 oxygen atoms.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is a primary source of energy for living organisms and is produced during photosynthesis in plants.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30876","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>How do veins ensure that blood flows in one direction in the human body?</span></p>","option_1":"By having thick, elastic walls","option_2":"By having a low blood pressure","option_3":"By contracting and relaxing","option_4":"By using valves","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - By using valves.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Veins contain one-way valves along their length that prevent the backflow of blood, ensuring that blood flows in only one direction — toward the heart — even though venous blood pressure is relatively low.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>These valves are especially important in the veins of the limbs, where blood must flow against gravity; malfunction of these valves can lead to conditions like varicose veins.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30877","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:10pt\"><span>In which year (Adid Harshavardhana ascend the throne?</span></p>","option_1":"515","option_2":"606","option_3":"698","option_4":"728","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 606.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Harshavardhana ascended the throne of Thanesar (and later Kannauj) in 606 CE, following the death of his brother Rajyavardhana.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>He went on to establish a large empire in northern India and was known for his patronage of learning and Buddhism; his reign is well documented in the accounts of the Chinese traveller Xuanzang (Hiuen Tsang) and the court poet Banabhatta's 'Harshacharita'.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30878","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following pair regarding Buddhism is correct?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Mahayana – New way of thinking</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Hinayana – Older Tradition</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Buddhism split into two major schools: Hinayana ('Lesser Vehicle'), representing the older, more conservative tradition close to the original teachings of the Buddha, and Mahayana ('Greater Vehicle'), which emerged later as a newer, more liberal school of thought emphasising the Bodhisattva ideal.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Both statements correctly characterise the two schools, making 'Both I and II' the correct answer.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30879","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Kwak Jatra' of Manipur is celebrated in the month of _____.</span></p>","option_1":"January","option_2":"March","option_3":"October","option_4":"December","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - October.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Kwak Jatra (also known as Kwak Tanba) is a traditional Meitei festival of Manipur, celebrated in the month of October (Mera in the Manipuri calendar), marking the harvest season.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The festival involves offerings and rituals connected to agricultural traditions and is an important part of Manipur's cultural calendar.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30880","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>At which of the following places was the Hindu College established in 1791 to encourage the study of ancient Sanskrit texts?</span></p>","option_1":"Calcutta","option_2":"Benaras","option_3":"Poona","option_4":"Mysore","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Benaras.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Hindu College (later the Sanskrit College) was established at Benaras (Varanasi) in 1791 by Jonathan Duncan, the British Resident, with the aim of encouraging the study of Hindu law, philosophy, and ancient Sanskrit texts.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This institution should not be confused with the Hindu College of Calcutta, founded later in 1817, which promoted Western/English education.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30881","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Non-Cooperation-Khilafat Movement began in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"April 1919","option_2":"January 1921","option_3":"February 1922","option_4":"December 1922","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - January 1921.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Non-Cooperation Movement, launched by Mahatma Gandhi in conjunction with the Khilafat Movement, was formally launched in January 1921, after being approved at the Congress session in Nagpur (December 1920).</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The joint movement aimed to unite Hindus and Muslims against British rule, combining the Khilafat cause (restoring the Ottoman Caliph's authority) with the broader nationalist struggle for self-governance (Swaraj).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30882","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Bhawaiya' folk songs, sung solo or in chorus, are traditionally associated with which Indian state?</span></p>","option_1":"Rajasthan","option_2":"Kerala","option_3":"West Bengal","option_4":"Andhra Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - West Bengal.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bhawaiya is a traditional folk music genre associated primarily with the northern regions of West Bengal (particularly the Cooch Behar/Duars region), as well as parts of Assam and Bangladesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>These songs traditionally express themes of rural life, love, and separation, often sung by cart drivers, farmers, and boatmen, and are recognised for their distinctive high-pitched vocal style.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30883","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the theme of 'TRIPS 2025' summit?</span></p>","option_1":"Bridging Research, Innovation, and Policy for a Sustainable Future","option_2":"Quantum Computing Advancements","option_3":"AI in Healthcare","option_4":"Renewable Energy Solutions","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Bridging Research, Innovation, and Policy for a Sustainable Future.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The theme of the TRIPS 2025 summit was 'Bridging Research, Innovation, and Policy for a Sustainable Future', reflecting its focus on connecting scientific research and innovation with policy-making toward sustainable development goals.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Such summits typically bring together researchers, policymakers, and industry leaders to discuss actionable strategies for sustainability-linked innovation.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30884","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following colloids has dispersed phase as liquid and dispersing phase as solid?</span></p>","option_1":"Fog","option_2":"Shaving cream","option_3":"Jelly","option_4":"Milk","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Jelly.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Jelly is an example of a gel, a type of colloid in which the dispersed phase is a liquid and the dispersing (continuous) medium is a solid.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In contrast, fog is a liquid dispersed in a gas, shaving cream is a gas dispersed in a liquid (foam), and milk is a liquid dispersed in a liquid (emulsion) — making jelly the correct match for a liquid-in-solid colloid.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30885","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which country organisation has produced the world's first carbon-14 diamond battery, with potential to provide power for thousands of years?</span></p>","option_1":"Japan","option_2":"Germany","option_3":"United States","option_4":"United Kingdom","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - United Kingdom.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A UK-based organisation developed the world's first carbon-14 diamond battery, which converts radioactive decay energy from carbon-14 into electricity, encased within a synthetic diamond, with the potential to provide low-level power for thousands of years without recharging.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This technology is significant for powering devices in situations where replacing batteries is impractical, such as space missions, medical implants, or remote sensors.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30886","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Rabi cropping season begins in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"March-April","option_2":"May-June","option_3":"July-August","option_4":"October-November","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - October-November.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Rabi cropping season in India typically begins in October-November (with the onset of winter) and the crops are harvested in March-April; major Rabi crops include wheat, barley, mustard, and gram.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This is distinct from the Kharif season (sown with the onset of monsoon, roughly June-July, and harvested around September-October), which grows crops like rice, maize, and cotton.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30887","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In the context of Balance on current account, which of the following is included in Invisibles?</span></p>","option_1":"Services","option_2":"Foreign direct investment","option_3":"Foreign institutional investment","option_4":"External Borrowings","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Services.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In the Balance of Payments, the current account records 'Invisibles', which include trade in services (like software, tourism, banking), income (interest, dividends), and unilateral transfers (remittances) — as opposed to visible trade in physical goods.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Foreign direct investment, foreign institutional investment, and external borrowings are all capital account transactions, not part of the current account's invisibles.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30888","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following is central problem of economy?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. What is produced and in what quantities?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. How are these goods produced?</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The central problems of an economy, as identified in economics, are: what to produce and in what quantities, how to produce it, and for whom to produce — arising from the basic issue of scarce resources having alternative uses.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Both 'what is produced and in what quantities' and 'how are these goods produced' are indeed among the central problems every economy must address, making 'Both I and II' correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30889","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following activity does NOT contribute to the land degradation?</span></p>","option_1":"Deforestation","option_2":"Contour farming","option_3":"Over grazing","option_4":"Mining and quarrying","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Contour farming.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Contour farming is a soil conservation technique in which crops are planted along the natural contours of land (following elevation lines), which helps reduce soil erosion by slowing down water runoff — it does NOT contribute to land degradation.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Deforestation, overgrazing, and mining/quarrying, on the other hand, are all well-known human activities that actively degrade land through erosion, loss of vegetation cover, and disruption of soil structure.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30890","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the author of the book 'The Calcutta Chromosome'?</span></p>","option_1":"Hari Kunzru","option_2":"Amitav Ghosh","option_3":"Pankaj Mishra","option_4":"Vikram Chandra","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Amitav Ghosh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Calcutta Chromosome' is a science fiction novel written by Amitav Ghosh, published in 1995, which won the Arthur C. Clarke Award for Best Science Fiction Novel.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The novel blends elements of history, mystery, and science fiction, revolving around research into the malaria parasite in colonial Calcutta.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30891","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which year service tax was introduced in India?</span></p>","option_1":"1991","option_2":"1994","option_3":"2005","option_4":"2008","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1994.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Service tax was introduced in India in 1994 through the Finance Act, 1994, initially levied on a limited set of services (like telephone, general insurance, and stockbroking), and was gradually expanded to cover a wider range of services over the following years.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Service tax was eventually subsumed into the Goods and Services Tax (GST), which came into effect in July 2017.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30892","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Between 2014–25, India attracted FDI worth USD _____ billion.</span></p>","option_1":"348.78","option_2":"548.78","option_3":"748.78","option_4":"948.78","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 748.78.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Between 2014 and 2025, India attracted cumulative Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) worth approximately USD 748.78 billion, reflecting the country's growing attractiveness as an investment destination following economic reforms and initiatives like 'Make in India'.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This sustained FDI inflow has been driven by sectors such as services, computer software and hardware, telecommunications, and automobiles.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30893","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Draupadi Ratha is a _____ type of temple.</span></p>","option_1":"chaitya","option_2":"constructed","option_3":"elephant-back type","option_4":"rock cut","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - rock cut.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Draupadi Ratha, part of the famous Pancha Rathas monument complex at Mahabalipuram (Mamallapuram), Tamil Nadu, is a rock-cut type of temple, carved directly out of a single large granite boulder during the Pallava dynasty (7th century CE).</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is designed in the form of a simple thatched hut and is dedicated to Goddess Durga, representing early Dravidian architectural style.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30894","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Farmers in northern India used to grow legumes as fodder in one season and wheat in the next season. This helped in the replenishment of the soil with _____.</span></p>","option_1":"sodium","option_2":"oxygen","option_3":"nitrogen","option_4":"hydrogen","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - nitrogen.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Legumes (such as gram, peas, and other pulses) have root nodules containing nitrogen-fixing bacteria (Rhizobium) that convert atmospheric nitrogen into a usable form, enriching the soil with nitrogen.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>This traditional crop rotation practice — growing legumes followed by cereal crops like wheat — helps naturally replenish soil nitrogen levels, reducing the need for chemical fertilisers and maintaining soil fertility.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30895","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"824","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Bhankora' musical instrument is related with which of the following state?</span></p>","option_1":"Tamil Nadu","option_2":"Rajasthan","option_3":"Kerala","option_4":"Uttarakhand","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Uttarakhand.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bhankora is a traditional long, curved wind (trumpet-like) musical instrument associated with the state of Uttarakhand, commonly played during religious ceremonies, temple rituals, and festive occasions in the Himalayan region.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>It is typically made of metal (copper or brass) and produces a deep, resonant sound, often used alongside other traditional instruments like the Ransingha and Dhol-Damau.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]